RFC 3986 (STD 66) -- Uniform Resource Identifier (URI): Generic Syntax
T. Berners-Lee, R. Fielding, L. Masinter. January 2005.

SOURCE
  https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986.txt
  vendored verbatim at test/vendor/rfc3986.txt
  sha256 3102dae4b68cebe40337730312fcb612297b8928547267e8b3d1ee6002b2d683

Nothing in this directory is authored. Every file beside this one is DERIVED
from the text above by cmake/UriCorpus.cmake; `make corpus-refresh` rewrites
them and the lane uri.harness.corpus-provenance re-derives and compares, so a
hand edit to a vector reddens rather than surviving (decision 0005 item 2).

WHAT IS TAKEN, AND FROM WHERE

  resolution.scm    section 5.4 -- Reference Resolution Examples.
                    23 rows from 5.4.1 "Normal Examples" and 19 from 5.4.2
                    "Abnormal Examples", all against the base section 5.4
                    declares: http://a/b/c/d;p?q
                    This is the only normative vector set this project has,
                    and M6 is gated on all 42 rows passing (decision 0011
                    item 3).

  dot-segments.scm  section 5.2.4 -- remove_dot_segments, worked by hand on
                    two inputs. Taken as STEP TRACES, 8 rows and 5, not only
                    as the two end results: the end result of the first is
                    reachable by several wrong algorithms and the trace is
                    not.

  example-uris.scm  section 1.1.2 -- eight example URIs across ftp, http,
                    ldap, mailto, news, tel, telnet and urn. These carry no
                    expected decomposition; the RFC gives them as
                    illustrations, so the only thing a lane may assert over
                    them is that they parse (decision 0005 item 3).

  rfc3986.abnf      Appendix A -- the Collected ABNF, 36 rules over 60 lines.
                    Not a vector set: this is the GRAMMAR, and decision 0012
                    makes it the source of the generated positive corpus, on
                    the observation that an ABNF is not only an acceptor but a
                    generator. Only the three-space indent and the page
                    furniture are removed; a continuation line keeps its
                    remaining indent, because that is what makes it a
                    continuation.

                    It is NOT self-contained. RFC 3986 leaves ALPHA, DIGIT and
                    HEXDIG to RFC 5234 Appendix B, so a generator needs those
                    three core rules alongside it. They are not restated here:
                    restating them would make this something other than what
                    the RFC says.

ERRATA

  Two errata are VERIFIED against RFC 3986 and neither touches this corpus:
  2525 (section 3.1, a reference update) and 7590 (section 5.2.4, a stray
  hyperlink).

  Erratum 4547 would rewrite two rows of section 5.4.2 -- "../../../g" and
  "../../../../g" -- from http://a/g to http://a/../g and http://a/../../g.
  It is "Held for Document Update", which is not normative text, and decision
  0015 item 2 rejects it: section 5.2.4 step 2C removes the last segment "and
  its preceding '/' (if any)", and section 5.4.2's own prose warns about
  exactly this case. The rows here are as published.

  Erratum 5428 claims the ABNF excludes "/" as a valid URI reference.
  path-absolute = "/" [ segment-nz *( "/" segment ) ] makes everything after
  the slash optional, so it does not. Decision 0015 item 3.

  Four further errata are open against sections this project will implement
  (4789 and 2933 on merge, 2033 on path-empty, 4942 on reg-name) and are
  adjudicated at their milestone, before the code they concern is written.

WHAT IS NOT HERE

  Vectors this project writes itself are a separate set, separately labelled,
  and are never counted in the same number as these (decision 0005 item 3).
  Generated corpora -- abnfgen over Appendix A, and its mutations -- are
  decision 0012's, and WPT's input-only corpus is decision 0013's. Provenance
  that is averaged is provenance destroyed.
